\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_crossmodel.pdf}
\caption{Cross-training-paradigm comparison of the median severity difference under importance-based eviction ($n{=}496$/$475$). Both DeepSeek (solid) and Qwen (dashed) exhibit monotonically increasing $\Delta$ with sparsity; Qwen's magnitude is approximately one-quarter to one-sixth of DeepSeek's.}
\label{fig:cross-model}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Per-problem concordance.}
The per-problem concordance across models is not significant (mean $\rho = -0.107$, $p = 0.926$), indicating that while the aggregate gradient direction replicates, the specific problems driving severity differ between models---consistent with model-specific attention patterns rather than a universal per-problem vulnerability ranking.

\paragraph{Random eviction control.}
As a control, random eviction on Qwen mirrors DeepSeek: non-significant at $s \leq 0.20$ ($\Delta$: $-0.01$, $0.02$; CIs span zero) but significant at $s \geq 0.25$ ($\Delta{=}0.39$, $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-10}$; $\Delta{=}0.91$, $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 10^{-13}$). Where significant, the random phase gradient is $2.7$--$3.5{\times}$ weaker than importance-based ($0.39$ vs.\ $1.07$ at $s{=}0.25$; $0.91$ vs.\ $3.16$ at $s{=}0.30$), confirming strategy-specific amplification.


\section{Permutation Test Details}
\label{app:permutation-details}

For each problem $i$ and sparsity level $s$, we compute the compound severity difference $\Delta_i^{(\text{strat})} = \mathrm{II}_{\textit{low},i} - \mathrm{II}_{\textit{high},i}$ under each eviction strategy. The test statistic is the median of the paired difference $\widetilde{\Delta} = \text{median}_i\!\bigl(\Delta_i^{(\text{imp})} - \Delta_i^{(\text{rand})}\bigr)$, measuring whether importance-based eviction produces systematically larger phase-dependent severity than random eviction. We permute strategy labels $10{,}000$ times to construct the null distribution and apply Bonferroni correction for 4 sparsity-level comparisons.

Per-problem distributions of the paired severity difference $\Delta_i = \mathrm{II}_{\textit{low},i} - \mathrm{II}_{\textit{high},i}$ under importance-based eviction are right-skewed at all sparsity levels, with mean/median ratios ranging from $1.2{\times}$ ($s{=}0.15$) to $1.6{\times}$ ($s{=}0.30$) for DeepSeek and $1.1{\times}$ to $1.4{\times}$ for Qwen.

The permutation null distributions are approximately symmetric around zero at all sparsity levels. At $s{=}0.15$, the observed statistic falls at the $99.92$th percentile of the null ($p_{\text{Bonf}} = 0.0032$); at $s \geq 0.20$, no permutation exceeds the observed statistic.

Table~\ref{tab:permutation} reports the results. All four sparsity levels reject $H_0$ after Bonferroni correction ($p_{\text{Bonf}} < 0.005$), with observed median differences increasing monotonically from $0.37$ at $s{=}0.15$ to $9.85$ at $s{=}0.30$. The monotonic growth of both the point estimate and the CI lower bound ($0.19 \to 0.69 \to 1.66 \to 6.70$) confirms that compound severity amplification strengthens with sparsity---not merely in central tendency but also in worst-case lower bounds. Figure~\ref{fig:permtest-forest} presents these results as a forest plot.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Paired permutation test for compound severity: importance vs.\ random eviction on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B ($n{=}49$ profiling subset, $10{,}000$ permutations, Bonferroni-corrected). The monotonic $\Delta$ pattern is confirmed at $n{=}496$ (Table~\ref{tab:aggregate-ii}).}
\label{tab:permutation}
\begin{tabular}{@{}rrcl@{}}
\toprule
$s$ & Observed $\widetilde{\Delta}$ & 95\% CI & $p_{\text{Bonf}}$ \\
\midrule
0.15 & 0.37 & $[0.19,\; 0.62]$ & 0.0032 \\
0.20 & 0.97 & $[0.69,\; 1.49]$ & ${<}\,0.001$ \\
0.25 & 3.34 & $[1.66,\; 6.64]$ & ${<}\,0.001$ \\
0.30 & 9.85 & $[6.70,\; 16.29]$ & ${<}\,0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_bootstrap.pdf}
\caption{Forest plot of permutation test results. Horizontal bars show 95\% permutation CIs; diamonds mark observed median differences $\widetilde{\Delta}$. All CIs lie entirely above zero, confirming significant strategy-dependent severity at every sparsity level.}
\label{fig:permtest-forest}
\end{figure}


\section{Cross-Architecture Analysis}
\label{app:cross-arch}

To test whether the phase-modulated severity pattern extends beyond the Qwen2 architecture, we replicate the experiment on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LlamaForCausalLM, 8B parameters; $n{=}49$ paired problems). Table~\ref{tab:llama-cross-arch} reports the strategy-dependent severity comparison across sparsity levels.

The evidence for a strategy contrast on LLaMA is mixed: at $s{=}0.15$, the bootstrap CI excludes zero ($[0.002, 0.043]$) but the Bonferroni-corrected Wilcoxon test is non-significant ($p_{\text{Bonf}}{=}0.219$); conversely, at $s{=}0.30$ the CI spans zero ($[-0.069, 0.544]$) while the Wilcoxon test reaches significance ($p_{\text{Bonf}}{=}0.029$). At intermediate levels ($s \in \{0.20, 0.25\}$), neither CI nor Wilcoxon reaches significance. The median paired difference increases monotonically ($\widetilde{\Delta}$: $0.020 \to 0.356$), consistent with a genuine but weak trend---partially confirming the cross-architecture hypothesis while underscoring the substantially weaker interaction on LlamaForCausalLM.\footnote{The CI/Wilcoxon discrepancy reflects differing power profiles under small $n$ with heavy-tailed distributions: the percentile bootstrap CI is based on the resampled median distribution and is less sensitive to tail observations, while the Wilcoxon signed-rank test uses signed ranks and is more sensitive to tail outliers. This inconsistency itself supports the conservative ``mixed/weak evidence'' interpretation.}

The absolute magnitude is architecture-dependent: LLaMA severity differences are ${\sim}40{\times}$ smaller than DeepSeek and ${\sim}7{\times}$ smaller than Qwen. Notably, random eviction on LLaMA also exhibits a non-trivial phase gradient ($d_z$: $0.35 \to 0.44$), substantially reducing the strategy contrast that is the hallmark of the phase-strategy interaction on Qwen2 models. This suggests that the strength of the strategy$\times$phase interaction---not merely the severity gradient itself---is architecture-dependent. The directional pattern---importance producing larger phase gradients than random---is qualitatively consistent across all three models, but the LLaMA evidence should be interpreted as preliminary given the weak interaction magnitude and partial statistical significance.

